# Code blocks

@lede
Triple-backtick fences. Optional language tag. Optional minification
attribute. Verbatim content.
@end

## The fence

```rust
fn main() {
    println!("hi");
}
```

The opening line is exactly three backticks, optionally followed by a
language tag and attributes. The closing line is exactly three backticks
on a line by themselves.

## Rules

- Opening and closing fences are exactly three backticks. Tilde fences
  (`~~~`) are *not* supported.
- The language tag is one ASCII identifier. It informs HTML class names
  (`<code class="language-rust">`) and LLM-mode minification.
- Content between the fences is verbatim — no escapes, no inline parsing.
- An unterminated fence is @ec[B0305] at end of file.
- Indented (four-space-prefix) code blocks are *not* supported.

## Showing source without a real fence

Because the closing fence is just three backticks, you cannot literally
nest one fence inside another. To document the syntax of a code fence
without rendering it as a real block, describe it in prose or use the
`@code` block shortcode form, which produces the same output as a fence.

## Minification attributes

Two attributes extend the opening fence:

@dl
`@nominify`
: Force the block to be emitted verbatim in LLM mode, even when its
  language is in the `minify_languages` allowlist. Useful when the
  formatting itself is the lesson.
`@minify-keep-comments`
: Strip whitespace but preserve comments. The compiler converts `//` to
  `/* */` where safe and emits a `B0703` warning per conversion.
@end

A `python` / `py` / `yaml` / `yml` / `makefile` block forced to minify is
@ec[B0704] *"language uses significant whitespace and cannot be safely
minified"* — these languages are permanently refused.

## In LLM mode

Each fenced block is processed independently:

@t
| Language | Default behavior in `--target=llm`
| `json` / `jsonl` | Minified to a single line
| `js`, `ts`, `rust`, `c`, `cpp`, `java`, `go`, `sql` | Minified
| `python`, `yaml`, `makefile` | Refused, emitted verbatim, `B0704`
| Untagged | Verbatim — without a tag the compiler can't safely process

JS / TS and Go preserve newlines (their automatic semicolon insertion
makes whitespace-stripping ambiguous without a real parser). C / C++
preserve newlines around `#`-preprocessor lines. Other languages collapse
to a single line.

## A tour of the three states

State 1 — full minification (default for known languages):

```rust
fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {
    // Adds two numbers.
    a + b
}
```

In LLM mode this becomes `fn add(a:i32,b:i32)->i32{a+b}`.

State 2 — verbatim. Add `@nominify` after the language tag in the opening
fence to keep the block exactly as written.

State 3 — strip whitespace, keep comments. Add `@minify-keep-comments`
after the language tag. Each `//` line comment converts to `/* */` form
with a `B0703` warning.

See @ref[llm-mode/code-minification.brf](Code-block minification) for the
full breakdown.

## Why JSON is the cleanest case

A 30-line pretty-printed JSON object is identical in meaning to its
single-line form. An LLM consumes both equally well. Token bills are paid
in the present, and JSON-heavy documents see >50 percent reduction in LLM
mode. That is the durable advantage Markdown can't ship without breaking
changes.
